                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,0)
          %h3#twice_differentiability_versus_quadratic_approximation Twice differentiability versus quadratic approximation
          %p
            While differentiability means approximability by a linear function, twice differentiability does
            %em not
            mean approximability by a quadratic function. For example, the function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 3
                  %mi sin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = x^3 \sin(1/x)
          %p
            (with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0) =0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(x) = 0
            in the sense that their difference is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi o
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o(x^2)
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\to 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                  %mi sin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = e^{-1/x^2} \sin(e^{1/x^2})
          %p
            is not twice differentiable at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=0
            %em any
            finite degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (namely, the zero polynomial), in the sense that their difference is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi o
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o(x^n)
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\to 0
          %p
            In some contexts, it is useful to say that functions such as these have “pointwise second derivatives”. More precisely, we say that a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            has a
            %strong
              pointwise
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi k
                      %mi th
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^{th}
              derivative
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            if it is continuous at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and there exists a polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo →
                      %mi a
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi a
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim_{x\to a} \frac{f(x) - p(x)}{(x-a)^k} = 0.
          %p
            (We have to state continuity explicitly in order to match the usual notion of differentiability when
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 1
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi k
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^{th}
            derivative is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi f
                    %mi pt
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k)}_{pt}(a) = p^{(k)}(a)
            (and it follows that all lower-order pointwise derivatives match as well). Thus we would say that while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 3
                  %mi sin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = x^3 \sin(1/x)
            does not have a second derivative at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %em pointwise
            second derivative at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi pt
                    %mo ″
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{pt}''(0) = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                  %mi sin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{-1/x^2} \sin(e^{1/x^2})
            is pointwise smooth. See e.g.
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://math.stackexchange.com/a/160383/91608"} this MSE answer
          %p
            The polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            may be thought of as the
            = succeed "th-order" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+polynomial"} Taylor polynomial
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt
            can be thought of as standing for ‘Peano–Taylor’ as well as for ‘pointwise’.) Note that while
            = succeed "-times" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-times" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
